# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.ty_cache/

# mise
# (mise.lock IS committed — reproducible tool installs)

# graphify-out: graph.json is DERIVED — regenerable from committed inputs
# (sources/*.manifest + sources/extractions/) via `mise run kb-build` (free, no
# LLM). Per CLAUDE.md invariant 3 and graphify's design, it is GITIGNORED: at
# aggregate scale it exceeds git/GitHub limits (382 MB / 364.7 MiB measured
# 2026-08-01 — the long-standing "119MB" here was ~3x stale, and two peer-tool
# reports disagreed 382 vs 119 because each inherited a different vintage of it),
# and consumers
# query via the MCP server (`mise run kb-serve`) or a pushed graph DB
# (neo4j/falkordb), never a git blob. manifest.json (change-detection) and
# .graphify_labels.json (labels keyed to NON-deterministic Louvain community ids
# — not reproducible across rebuilds, so they can't "ship together") are likewise
# derived/coupled and gitignored. COMMITTED = reproducible inputs + authored
# graphify-out/memory/ (work-memory). Other views regenerate via `kb-artifacts`.
graphify-out/graph.json
# graph-prose.json is a pure function of graph.json (kb_setup.prose), so it is
# derived twice over — committing it would pin a scoped corpus to whatever the
# unscoped one looked like on the day of the commit.
graphify-out/graph-prose.json
graphify-out/manifest.json
# The aggregate WITHOUT our own code — `kb-build` writes it, `kb-watch` restarts
# from it so repeated refreshes are idempotent. Derived and roughly graph.json-
# sized (~345MB), so it is ignored for both of the reasons graph.json is. NOT
# covered by the `.graphify_*` rule below: that glob is graphify's own state,
# whereas this file is ours.
graphify-out/.base-graph.json
# `scope = study` sources — repos we ANALYSE rather than learn from. Derived
# (87 MiB, rebuilt by `kb-build` from the same pinned manifests as everything
# else) and kept out of the aggregate because merging it in took graph.json past
# graphify's 512 MiB cap.
graphify-out/study-graph.json
graphify-out/cache/
graphify-out/transcripts/
graphify-out/converted/
graphify-out/*.html
graphify-out/*.svg
graphify-out/*.graphml
graphify-out/cypher.txt
graphify-out/wiki/
graphify-out/obsidian/
graphify-out/GRAPH_REPORT.md
graphify-out/.graphify_*
# .currency-stamp.json fingerprints THIS machine's last build (version + artifact
# stats), so it is per-clone state, not a shared input: committing it would make
# every rebuild a diff and every parallel clone a conflict, and a fresh clone
# would inherit a stamp asserting a build that never happened there. Its absence
# is the correct first-run state — `kb-setup currency check` reports "rebuild
# pending" until a local `kb-build` writes it. Added 2026-07-26, the first run
# that actually produced the file (the stamp mechanism shipped without its own
# ignore rule, so nothing had revealed the gap).
graphify-out/.currency-stamp.json
# committed under graphify-out/: ONLY memory/ (authored work-memory).
# graph.json / manifest.json / .graphify_* (labels, learning overlay) are derived.

# graphify per-install state (generated by `graphify install`/runs; not source)
.claude/skills/graphify/.graphify_version
.claude/skills/**/.graphify_root

# External REPOS — cloned on demand from sources/*.manifest at a pinned SHA, NEVER
# committed. Non-repo sources (video transcripts, docs, PDFs) that CAN'T be
# deterministically re-fetched ARE vendored under sources/media/.
sources/*/
!sources/extractions/
!sources/media/
!sources/.gitkeep
sources/media/*.m4a       # audio — the transcript is the committed artifact
sources/media/*.pdf.txt   # regenerable from the committed .pdf via pypdf

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
# Agent session state (OMC HUD etc.) — per-session scratch, never source. Left
# untracked-but-unignored it is one `git add .` away from being committed.
.agent/
# `.omc/` stays ignored even though the convention moved to `.agent/`: the
# statusline HUD configured in the USER-level ~/.claude/settings.json
# (`node $HOME/.claude/hud/omc-hud.mjs`) still writes `.omc/state/` into
# whichever repo is cwd, and it recreates the directory within seconds of
# deletion. Dropping this line would dirty the tree on every session. Removing
# it for real is a ~/.claude/settings.json change — user-level config this repo
# does not edit.
.omc/
# reflect derived doc (regenerable via `graphify reflect`); memory/ IS tracked (authored)
graphify-out/reflections/
# graphify dated backup dirs (redundant with git history)
graphify-out/20[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]/
# transient fetch cache for refetchable prose (mintlify docs etc.) — the durable
# artifact is the extraction chunk (records source_url + captured_at), not the raw md
sources/raw/
# graphify `add`'s default fetch dir (./raw) — same policy: transient (fetched
# webpages + downloaded audio like yt_*.m4a); the extraction chunk is the artifact
/raw/

# THIS repo's own code, extracted into scoped sub-graphs by `kb_setup.graph`'s
# `_extract_self` and then merged into the aggregate. Ignored WHOLESALE, unlike the
# root and brain/ blocks above, and the difference is the point: those two trees
# each contain an authored `memory/` that must survive, so they can only ignore
# named derived artifacts. Nothing under these two is authored — every byte is
# regenerable by `mise run kb-build` — so a directory-level rule is both correct
# and immune to graphify adding a new output filename we would forget to list.
python/graphify-out/
tests/graphify-out/

# second-brain vault (brain/): the .md notes are the source of truth; the graph is
# DERIVED (regenerable via `graphify update`). Mirror the root graphify-out rules —
# ignore derived artifacts, commit ONLY the authored memory/ work-memory.
brain/graphify-out/graph.json
brain/graphify-out/manifest.json
brain/graphify-out/cache/
brain/graphify-out/*.html
brain/graphify-out/*.svg
brain/graphify-out/*.graphml
brain/graphify-out/cypher.txt
brain/graphify-out/wiki/
brain/graphify-out/obsidian/
brain/graphify-out/GRAPH_REPORT.md
brain/graphify-out/.graphify_*
brain/graphify-out/reflections/
brain/graphify-out/20[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]/
